paragraphs.en:
The green hills, ah, are deep green;
the blue pool, ah, is vast and deep.
The flying torrent, ah, rolls wide,
joining the Silver River of the sky.
There is someone, ah, in the midst of the waves,
shaking her girdle-jades, tinkling.
Majesty and flame, blazing and bright;
ah, the bearing is lofty.
A lucky day is chosen, ah, an hour of good omen;
the phoenix call is foretold, clear and ringing.
There are wings, ah, the splendid hall;
there is good omen, ah, long and numinous.
We summon a man of letters, ah, to make a short piece;
we sing the great transformation, ah, and raise the long beam.
Pour cassia wine, ah, fly the feathered cups;
light swallows turn and tread the spring sun.
The beast's mouth, ah, spouts auspicious incense;
the pig-robe, ah, boils the jade liquor.
Strike the fish-drum, ah, with a clang;
blow the dragon flute, ah, with quick steps.
The god sits solemn upon his couch;
looking up to his supreme virtue, ah, we cannot forget.

There is someone, ah, in the nook of the hill,
clad in fig-vine, belted with creeper.
The day is about to set, ah, on the clear waves,
which rise in fine ripples like gauze.
The wind sways, ah, her hair hangs loose;
clouds drift, ah, her robes billow.
She circles, ah, winding and gliding,
and passes with a skillful smile.
She casts off my jacket, ah, at the singing whirlpool;
unties my ring, ah, on the cold sand.
Dew wets, ah, the courtyard sedge;
mist darkens, ah, the high peaks.
Looking at the far peaks towering,
they are like blue snails on the river.
Sparse beats on the copper gong;
drunken dancing, ah, swaying and swaying.
There is wine like mud;
there is meat like a hill.
The guests are drunk, ah, red-faced;
they make new tunes, ah, and sing merrily.
Some support each other, drag each other;
some clap each other, laugh at each other.
Strike the jade pot and drink without end;
when pure delight is spent, ah, sad feelings are many.

The sea pays court with unceasing force,
rushing waves babble, bearing a light boat.
After the clouds first scatter, the moon sinks into the creek;
when the tide is about to rise, wind fills the isle.
On warm days tortoises and fish drift in and out;
on bright waves ducks float or sink at will.
Year after year striking the rocks, I sob much;
tonight's joy sweeps away the hundred cares.

Shadows of five-flowered trees shade the layered mats;
bamboo and wooden vessels and reed organs are laid out in order.
In the mica curtain songs turn lilting;
inside the crystal blinds the dancers hesitate and glide.
The divine dragon is no creature of the pool;
the man of letters has always been the jewel of the mat.
How could I tie the sun with a long rope
and linger, drunk as mud, in the bright spring?

The Divine King, tipsy, leans on his golden couch;
the mountain haze is dim, and it is already the evening sun.
In the marvellous dance, swaying, brocade sleeves turn;
in the clear song, softly, voices circle the carved beam.
For years the lonely indignation turned the silver isle;
today in joy together we raise the jade cups.
Light and shade flow away and people know it not;
past and present, worldly affairs, how swift and hurried!

Mt. Cheonma stands high above the Milky Way,
cliff-water flies far through the air.
Straight down it pierces the grove and gorge,
rushing, it makes a great torrent.
In the heart of the waves a moon-cave soaks;
at the pool's bottom lies the hidden dragon palace.
Transformation leaves divine traces,
soaring and seizing build great merit.
Haze and mist give birth to fine fog,
mild and sweeping, auspicious winds rise.
In the blue sky the tally is weighty;
in Cheonggu the ranks of nobles are high.
Riding cloud, he attends the Purple Pole;
driving rain, he harnesses the blue dappled horse.
In the golden gate a fine banquet opens;
on the jade steps a separate swan sings.
Flowing rosy mist floats on the tea bowls;
heavy dew drips on the red lotus.
Bowing and yielding, his dignity is weighty;
circling, his ritual measure is abundant.
Robes and caps, the writing brilliant;
girdle-jades ring, tinkling.
Fish and turtles come to pay court and congratulate;
rivers and streams assemble together.
How dazzling the numinous mechanism!
His dark virtue deeper still and serene.
In the garden drums are beaten to hasten the flowers;
cups tip, drinking like a rainbow.
Heavenly maidens blow jade flutes;
the Queen Mother tunes silk and paulownia.
A hundred bows pass the sweet wine;
three shouts wish him long life like Mt. Hua and Mt. Song.
Smoke sinks on the frost-and-snow fruit;
the plates reflect crystal onions.
Rare flavours moisten the throat;
the wave of grace soaks to the bone.
It is like eating the mist and dew
and seems to have arrived at Yingpeng.
Joy ends, and we shall part;
elegance is a dream.
